const sections = [
{id:'summary',group:'Profile',title:'Summary',meta:'Your opening story',resume:[`Full-Stack Software Engineer with experience building responsive web applications and maintaining production websites. Skilled in JavaScript, TypeScript, React, Next.js, Node.js, REST APIs, SQL, and modern databases, with an emphasis on thoughtful product design and reliable implementation. Experienced in collaborating with technical and creative teams to ideate concepts and transform it into polished digital products.`],topics:[['Your engineering story','Prepare a 60–90 second introduction connecting your degree, CMS work, full-stack projects, and target role. Explain the transition from hospitality through concrete transferable skills.'],['Full-stack architecture','Trace a user action from React UI through an HTTP request, backend validation, database operation, and response. Identify where your own projects use each layer.'],['Product design and reliability','Discuss responsive layouts, accessibility, error and loading states, testing, and maintenance. Bring an example of a user need that changed your implementation.'],['Ownership and collaboration','Distinguish what you designed, implemented, tested, and deployed personally from work shared with teammates or AI tools.']],questions:['Tell me about yourself and how you became a software engineer.','Which project best demonstrates your readiness for this role?','Walk me through a feature from the browser to the database.','What does thoughtful product design mean in a project you built?','Tell me about a production issue you diagnosed and resolved.','Why this role, and what do you want to learn next?'],focus:'Rehearse a concise introduction, one technical success, and one mistake you learned from.'},
{id:'skills',group:'Profile',title:'Skills',meta:'Languages · Frontend · Backend · Databases · Tools',resume:[`Languages: JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, SQL`,`Frontend: React, Next.js, HTML/CSS, Tailwind CSS, Responsive Design`,`Backend: Node.js, Express, REST APIs, Authentication, WebSockets`,`Databases: PostgreSQL, MongoDB`,`Tools: Git, GitHub Actions, Docker, Vitest, Playwright, ChatGPT Codex, Software Testing, Agent Harness Development`],topics:[['JavaScript and TypeScript','Explain scope, closures, reference vs. value, array methods, promises, async/await, error propagation, and the event loop. Know interfaces/types, unions, narrowing, generics, and why TypeScript does not validate runtime input.'],['React','Explain props/state, rendering, keys, controlled forms, hooks, effect dependencies and cleanup, lifting state, and context. Show how you prevent stale state, unnecessary effects, and inaccessible interactions.'],['Next.js','Explain routing, client/server boundaries, rendering options, data fetching, and backend endpoints in the router/version you actually used. Distinguish React from the framework features Next.js adds.'],['HTML, CSS, and Tailwind','Discuss semantic HTML, keyboard navigation, labels, the box model, flex/grid, breakpoints, and responsive testing. Explain utility classes and how you keep styles maintainable.'],['Node.js, Express, and REST','Explain middleware ordering, request validation, centralized error handling, async I/O, HTTP methods/status codes, idempotency, pagination, and consistent API contracts.'],['Authentication and WebSockets','Distinguish authentication from authorization; explain session lifecycle, cookies, password hashing, CSRF/XSS risks, and HTTP vs. persistent bidirectional connections.'],['PostgreSQL, MongoDB, and SQL','Practice joins, grouping, constraints, indexes, transactions, schema design, embedding vs. referencing, and explaining query performance. Compare relational and document models using your own use cases.'],['Python','Review collections, comprehensions, functions, virtual environments, exception handling, and the data-processing tools used in your market analysis.'],['Git and delivery','Explain branches, commits, pull requests, merge conflicts, reverts, CI triggers, Docker images vs. containers, environment variables, and how secrets stay out of source control.'],['Testing and AI tools','Compare unit, integration, and end-to-end tests; discuss mocks, test isolation, flaky tests, AI output review, context selection, and harness permissions.']],questions:['How do promises and async/await interact with the JavaScript event loop?','What does TypeScript catch, and what still needs runtime validation?','When should you use an effect in React, and when can you avoid one?','Why do list keys matter? How do you prevent stale state updates?','Which Next.js router did you use, and how did you choose client versus server code?','How would you make a form accessible and responsive?','How do you structure Express middleware and handle asynchronous errors?','When would you use PUT, PATCH, POST, and DELETE?','How do authentication and authorization differ?','When would you choose WebSockets over polling?','Write a SQL query that joins expenses to users and groups spending by month.','When would an index help, and what does it cost?','When would you choose PostgreSQL over MongoDB?','How do you resolve a Git merge conflict safely?','What belongs in a Docker image, and what should be supplied at runtime?','How do unit, integration, and end-to-end tests complement each other?','How do you verify code produced by an AI assistant?'],focus:'Every listed skill is fair game. For each, prepare one example of where you used it and an honest description of your depth.'},
{id:'education',group:'Background',title:'Education & Certifications',meta:'Computer science degree · AWS certification preparation',resume:[`B.A. in Information & Computer Science`,`Coursework: Machine Learning, Big Data Analysis, Database Systems, Software Engineering, Data Structures & Algorithms`,`AWS Certified Solutions Architect – Associate — Planned`],topics:[['Data structures and algorithms','Review arrays, hash maps, stacks, queues, trees, graphs, sorting/searching, and Big-O time/space complexity. Practice explaining a solution and testing edge cases aloud.'],['Database systems','Explain keys, normalization, relationships, joins, indexes, ACID transactions, and schema tradeoffs. Connect classroom concepts to your PostgreSQL and MongoDB projects.'],['Software engineering','Discuss requirements, modular design, version control, code review, Agile practices, testing, and working within a shared codebase.'],['ML and big data foundations','Explain supervised learning, regression, feature engineering, validation, overfitting, leakage, and what changes when data no longer fits in memory. Be clear about hands-on experience versus coursework.'],['AWS preparation','Describe what you have studied or built so far: IAM, networking, compute, storage, databases, availability, monitoring, and cost tradeoffs. The certification is planned, not yet earned; verify the exam version when you register.']],questions:['Which course most influenced how you build software?','How would you choose a data structure for a messaging room lookup?','Explain the time and space complexity of a solution you wrote.','What is normalization, and when might you denormalize?','What did your team learn from a software engineering project?','What causes overfitting or data leakage?','Why are you pursuing AWS certification, and what have you done so far?','At a high level, how would you host one of your applications on AWS?'],focus:'Keep the expected certification status explicit. Separate knowledge you studied from services you have actually operated.'},
{id:'hospitality',group:'Experience',title:'Hospitality Experience',meta:'Sushi Chef',resume:[`Consistently earned Employee of the Month for exceptional reliability, performance, and leading by example.`,`Excelled in a high pressure, unpredictable environment through anticipating and adapting to staffing shortages and challenges.`,`Trained over a dozen employees, and iterated on standard procedures for more efficient operations.`],topics:[['Behavioral stories using STAR','Prepare Situation, Task, Action, and Result examples for pressure, leadership, conflict, mistakes, and process improvement. Keep the action focused on your own decisions.'],['Prioritization under pressure','Explain how you triaged competing tasks, maintained quality, communicated constraints, and adapted when staffing changed. Connect this to engineering teamwork without overstating equivalence.'],['Training and process improvement','Describe how you taught a repeatable process, checked understanding, incorporated feedback, and improved a procedure. Bring a concrete before-and-after example.'],['Evidence behind claims','Know the actual award frequency, number of trainees, and observable results. Use exact numbers only if you can support them.']],questions:['Tell me about a time you performed under pressure.','How did you handle an unexpected staffing shortage?','Describe a conflict with a teammate and how you resolved it.','How did you train someone who was struggling?','What process did you improve, and how did you know it worked?','Tell me about a mistake you made and what changed afterward.','How will your hospitality experience help you on an engineering team?','Why are you moving into software engineering now?'],focus:'Prepare three reusable STAR stories with specific actions and truthful outcomes.'},
{id:'cmsWork',group:'Experience',title:'Entertainment Company',meta:'CMS Developer',resume:[`Served as the sole developer for a musical group’s official website, a 3D-based, mobile responsive, CMS website.`,`Owned the entire project from ideation to design and implementation using Webflow, Figma, Blender, GIMP, and DaVinci Resolve.`,`Designed the CMS around maintainability and enabling non-technical team members to manage content independently.`,`Collaborated with idol members and team members to develop a unified concept that aligned creative visions.`,`Managed website maintenance, feature updates, and troubleshooting across multiple Entertainment Company websites.`],topics:[['CMS architecture and Webflow','Explain collections, fields, references, reusable templates, content editing, publishing, and how you made changes safe for non-technical editors. Identify custom code versus platform features.'],['Design-to-build workflow','Trace requirements and Figma designs into Webflow layouts. Explain how Blender, GIMP, and DaVinci Resolve contributed actual assets. Clarify whether 3D meant rendered media, embedded interaction, or a live scene.'],['Responsive design and performance','Discuss mobile layouts, image/video compression, asset loading, reduced motion, accessibility, and tradeoffs between visual impact and page speed.'],['Maintenance and stakeholder management','Prepare examples of debugging, feedback rounds, scope decisions, content training, and handoff documentation. Be precise about sites you maintained versus built.']],questions:['Walk me through the musical group’s website from initial concept to launch.','Why did you choose Webflow rather than a custom React application?','How did you structure the CMS for non-technical editors?','What did “3D-based” mean technically?','How did you optimize large visual assets for mobile users?','How did you handle accessibility and responsive testing?','Describe a disagreement about the creative direction.','What was the hardest bug or maintenance issue you resolved?','Which parts did you build yourself, and which came from the platform?','What would you change if you rebuilt it today?'],focus:'Have a screenshot or live walkthrough ready, plus a clear explanation of the CMS model and your hardest design tradeoff.'},
{id:'messaging',group:'Projects',title:'Anonymous Real-Time Messaging Platform',meta:'React · TypeScript · Express · PostgreSQL · Docker · WebSockets · GitHub Actions',resume:[`Developed a real-time messaging platform with WebSocket connections, featuring room owner administrative controls.`,`Implemented anonymous sessions through HTTP-only session cookies, TTL-based room expiration, and automated data cleanup.`,`Built automated unit, API integration, and end-to-end tests using Vitest, Supertest, and Playwright, with validation executed through GitHub Actions.`],topics:[['Architecture and connection lifecycle','Draw the browser, Express API, WebSocket server, PostgreSQL tables, and cleanup job. Explain connection setup, room membership, disconnects, reconnects, and how HTTP requests share identity with sockets.'],['Anonymous sessions and authorization','Explain session ID generation/storage, cookie flags, expiry, server-side ownership checks, socket authentication, origin checks, CSRF considerations, and rate limiting. Anonymous sessions do not guarantee anonymity from operators or infrastructure.'],['Messaging correctness','Discuss message validation, ordering, duplicate handling, acknowledgement, missed messages, reconnect recovery, and what your implementation actually guarantees.'],['TTL and cleanup','Explain where expiry is stored, how expired rooms are rejected before physical deletion, scheduling, cascade deletion, races with active connections, and idempotent cleanup.'],['Testing, CI, and containers','Map Vitest to units, Supertest to HTTP integration, and Playwright to browser flows. Explain test databases, socket tests, timing-related flakiness, CI services, Docker networking, and configuration.'],['Scaling and abuse prevention','Be ready to reason about multiple server instances, cross-instance broadcasting, shared session state, connection limits, backpressure, payload limits, and denial-of-service controls. Label proposed improvements clearly.']],questions:['Walk me through sending a message end to end.','Why WebSockets instead of HTTP polling or server-sent events?','How is an anonymous visitor identified across requests and socket connections?','How do you prevent a non-owner from performing owner actions?','What does HttpOnly protect against, and what does it not prevent?','What happens if a room expires while people are connected?','How does cleanup run, and what happens if it fails or runs twice?','What happens when a user disconnects and reconnects?','Can messages arrive twice or out of order? What guarantees do you provide?','How do you validate messages and prevent spam?','How would you run this across multiple backend instances?','What did you test at each layer, and what bugs did the tests catch?','How does GitHub Actions start the database and execute your tests?','What is the largest limitation of the current design?'],focus:'Practice a five-minute architecture walkthrough and be ready to open the session, authorization, cleanup, and CI code.'},
{id:'budget',group:'Projects',title:'Budget Visualization Web App',meta:'React · Next.js · PostgreSQL · TypeScript · Chart.js · Vercel',resume:[`Built a full-stack budgeting app where users can set monthly budgets, add and edit expenses, and review spending history.`,`Created interactive doughnut and bar charts to compare spending against budgets, highlight overspending, and show monthly trends.`,`Implemented account registration and login with bcrypt password hashing, cookie-based sessions, and protected pages.`,`Developed API endpoints for CRUD operations with parameterized SQL and database transactions to prevent partial saves.`],topics:[['Application and data model','Sketch users, budgets, expenses, keys, relationships, constraints, and per-user ownership. Explain Next.js routing and where data is loaded, validated, and mutated.'],['Authentication and authorization','Explain registration, bcrypt hashing and verification, session creation/expiry/logout, cookie flags, protected pages, and independent server-side ownership checks on every endpoint.'],['SQL safety and transactions','Explain parameter binding versus string interpolation, transaction boundaries, rollback, concurrency, and the specific multi-step operation that requires all-or-nothing behavior. Parameterized SQL does not replace authorization.'],['Money and date correctness','Explain decimal or integer-cent storage, rounding, input validation, timezones, month boundaries, refunds, zero budgets, and preventing duplicate submissions.'],['Chart.js and state synchronization','Trace aggregation into chart datasets, updates after edits, empty/loading/error states, accessible alternatives, and interpretation of overspending and monthly trends.'],['Deployment on Vercel','Explain environment variables, database connectivity, migrations, runtime constraints, connection pooling, deployment verification, and handling secrets.']],questions:['Walk me through adding an expense and updating the chart.','What does your database schema look like?','How do you prevent one user from accessing another user’s expenses?','Why use bcrypt, and how does login verification work?','How are sessions stored, expired, and revoked on logout?','Show an example of a parameterized query and the attack it prevents.','Which operation needs a transaction, and what happens on failure?','What happens if two requests edit the same budget at once?','How do you represent currency and handle month boundaries?','How do charts handle empty data, zero budgets, and overspending?','How did you deploy and connect PostgreSQL on Vercel?','What would you change for more users or larger datasets?'],focus:'Be able to demonstrate a real transaction boundary, an ownership check, and a correct money calculation.'},
{id:'harness',group:'Projects',title:'AI Development Agent Harness',meta:'ChatGPT Codex',resume:[`Developed a learning-focused, role-based development harness with specialized workflows for validation, testing, styling, and review.`,`Configured the agent with guardrails, structured handoff instructions, and enforced test execution to reduce runaway behavior.`,`Reduced token usage by 60+%, through task-specific context loading and gstack-inspired agent roles.`],topics:[['Harness architecture','Explain the distinction between a model, agent, prompt, tool, workflow, and harness. Walk through role selection, task state, context loading, handoffs, and completion criteria.'],['Guardrails and enforcement','Describe tool permissions, allowed scope, stop conditions, retry limits, time/token budgets, and approval gates. Distinguish instructions requesting tests from executable checks that actually block completion.'],['Context engineering','Explain what each role receives, how files are selected, what is omitted, and how you prevent lost requirements or stale information between handoffs.'],['Evaluation and the 60+% claim','Prepare baseline and modified workflows, comparable tasks/models, token accounting, sample size, repeated runs, and quality checks. State whether the number is measured, estimated, or limited to a particular trial.'],['AI reliability and human review','Discuss hallucinated APIs, prompt injection from untrusted files, secrets, tool errors, independent review, and validating changes against requirements.'],['Original contribution and inspiration','Identify what you adapted from gstack-inspired roles and what you designed yourself. Bring configuration files or a workflow diagram; avoid implying you trained a model.']],questions:['What problem did your harness solve?','Walk me through one task from intake to validation and completion.','How did you decide which roles and workflows were needed?','What makes this a harness rather than a collection of prompts?','What are your guardrails, and which are technically enforced?','What happens if an agent skips tests or repeatedly fails?','How do you decide what context each role receives?','How exactly did you measure the 60+% token reduction?','Was output quality comparable, and how did you evaluate it?','What was inspired by gstack, and what did you build yourself?','Describe a failure or runaway case and the change it prompted.','When would you use one agent instead of several?'],focus:'The 60+% claim invites scrutiny. Bring reproducible logs or qualify the scope honestly; never invent a benchmark.'},
{id:'matching',group:'Projects',title:'Full-Stack Student Matching Platform',meta:'React · Meteor · MongoDB',resume:[`Collaborated in a shared codebase to develop study session scheduling, user profiles, messaging, and administrative features.`,`Applied Agile principles through feature-driven milestones and team coordination to minimize development bottlenecks.`],topics:[['Meteor architecture','Explain how React and Meteor fit together, how client/server data moves in the version you used, and whether you used publications/subscriptions, methods, or other APIs. Describe reactivity and access controls.'],['MongoDB modeling','Explain collections for profiles, sessions, and messages, embedding vs. references, indexes, input validation, and limiting which fields clients receive.'],['Scheduling and permissions','Discuss timezones, overlapping sessions, availability, membership, role checks, and protecting administrative actions on the server. Separate implemented behavior from design ideas.'],['Team engineering','Prepare examples of your exact contribution, feature milestones, pull requests, merge conflicts, code review, coordination, and a bottleneck you helped remove.']],questions:['Which features did you personally implement?','How did React, Meteor, and MongoDB work together?','How did data updates reach the UI?','How did you model users, sessions, and messages?','How were server operations and admin features authorized?','How did scheduling handle conflicting times or timezones?','How did you split work and integrate changes in a shared codebase?','Tell me about a merge conflict or technical disagreement.','What did Agile look like in your actual weekly workflow?','What would you improve in the architecture today?'],focus:'Use one feature to explain both technical ownership and collaboration. Be specific about the code you contributed.'},
{id:'aapl',group:'Projects',title:'AAPL Market Data Analysis',meta:'Python · NumPy · Matplotlib · Scikit-learn',resume:[`Analyzed historical AAPL market data to investigate relationships between technical indicators and stock-price movements.`,`Cleaned, transformed, and normalized time-series data and engineered technical indicators as features for predictive modeling.`,`Trained and evaluated multiple linear regression models to identify and assess strong indicators.`],topics:[['Dataset and target definition','Know the data source, date range, sampling frequency, adjustments, missing values, and exact target: price, return, or future movement. Clarify whether multiple models or multiple linear regression is meant.'],['Feature engineering','Explain the indicators you actually computed, their lookback windows, and why each might relate to the target. Check that every feature was available at the prediction timestamp.'],['Time-series validation and leakage','Explain chronological splits, training-only preprocessing, shifted targets, overlapping windows, walk-forward evaluation, and why random splits can give misleading results.'],['Linear regression','Explain coefficients, intercept, residuals, fitting objective, multicollinearity, outliers, overfitting, and limitations. Distinguish correlation, predictive usefulness, and causation.'],['Metrics and baselines','Know which metrics you used and why: MAE/RMSE, R², or other justified measures. Compare against a simple baseline such as predicting the last price, using the same split and horizon.'],['Python toolchain and communication','Describe NumPy transformations, scikit-learn fitting/preprocessing, Matplotlib plots, reproducibility, and how charts exposed patterns or failures. This project alone does not establish a profitable strategy.']],questions:['Where did your data come from, and what were you predicting?','Which indicators did you engineer and why?','How did you handle missing values and normalize features?','How did you split the data without leaking future information?','Were preprocessing steps fitted only on the training set?','Why choose linear regression? What assumptions or limitations mattered?','How did you evaluate the models and compare against a baseline?','What does a regression coefficient mean when features are correlated?','Which indicator was strongest, and how did you determine that?','How stable were the results across different time periods?','What can you conclude from the analysis, and what can’t you conclude?','What would you improve if you repeated the project?'],focus:'Bring the actual target definition, train/test dates, metrics, baseline, and one plot. Do not claim causation or trading performance without evidence.'}
];
